## 6. Language Gives the Imagined World Structure

Language and play become increasingly intertwined during toddlerhood.

Words allow children to name roles, negotiate rules, sequence events, represent intentions, and sustain narratives.

Play, in turn, creates reasons to talk.

A child needs language to say who is the doctor.

Another child needs language to reject the proposed role.

A shared story needs a sequence.

A disagreement requires negotiation.

An imagined object must sometimes be described.

The relationship between language and play is therefore plausibly reciprocal. Language makes more elaborate symbolic play possible, while play provides contexts in which communication can be used for increasingly complex purposes.

This does not mean that pretend play is a substitute for language development.

It is not.

Research has not established a durable, play-specific effect on general language ability, and interventions described as “play-based language” frequently include several active ingredients: shared reading, adult scaffolding, explicit vocabulary, phonological activities, narrative instruction, and conversational interaction.

The sensible conclusion is narrower and more useful:

Play is a good context in which language can be used.

That is different from saying that play itself is the treatment.

