## 8. What Happens When Play Meets Creativity?

Creativity is one of the areas in which enthusiasm has most easily outrun evidence.

Play certainly gives children opportunities to transform materials, generate alternatives, combine ideas, and produce novel representations.

But creativity is not simply novelty.

A child who produces something unusual is not necessarily demonstrating a general capacity for creative problem solving.

Assessment is influenced by language, familiarity, motivation, culture, adult expectations, and the way originality is scored. Measures may combine fluency, flexibility, novelty, narrative ability, and elaboration in different proportions.

The evidence therefore supports an opportunity claim more readily than a training claim.

Play can provide occasions for children to generate and revise possibilities.

It does not follow that more play—or more pretend play—produces greater creativity on unrelated tasks.

There is a difference between giving a child space to create and proving that the space transformed a general psychological capacity.

That distinction is characteristic of developmental science at its best.

