## 10. Digital Worlds

The modern child's play environment also includes digital materials.

This creates a recurring temptation to ask whether digital play is “good” or “bad.”

That is too crude a question.

Digital play is not one activity.

A child may use a tablet to watch passively, draw, construct, solve a problem, tell a story, collaborate with another person, simulate a world, or communicate socially. These activities can involve radically different cognitive and social processes.

The relevant variables are therefore not simply whether a screen is present or how many minutes have elapsed.

What is the child doing?

Who is participating?

How much agency does the child have?

What is the content?

What purpose does the activity serve?

What does it displace?

The displacement question is particularly important.

Time spent with a digital activity may also mean less time available for sleep, physical movement, conversation, hands-on exploration, or face-to-face play.

At the same time, digital play should not be treated as automatically empty or harmful. The evidence does not justify assuming that digital and physical play are equivalent, but neither does it justify treating all digital activity as one developmental exposure.

The medium matters.

The activity matters more.

