## 11. A Developmental Timeline

The emergence of play-related capacities is gradual.

During the first six months, infants increasingly investigate their bodies, movements, sounds, and objects. Exploration is intentional in important ways, but it should not be confused with symbolic play.

Between six and twelve months, object-directed activity becomes more varied, while social routines, imitation, gestures, and shared attention provide foundations for later representational abilities.

Between twelve and eighteen months, conventional uses of objects become more established, and early representational acts can appear in familiar routines.

Around eighteen to twenty-four months, spontaneous pretend play and object substitution commonly become visible.

Between twenty-four and thirty-six months, pretend scenarios, roles, language, narratives, and social coordination can become substantially more elaborate.

This is a progression, not a checklist.

Children do not pass through identical stages on identical dates.

Motor development, language, experience, interaction, temperament, opportunity, and culture all contribute to when and how a particular form of play becomes visible.

A milestone is useful when it describes a population tendency.

It becomes harmful when it is turned into a judgment about an individual child.

