## 13. The Child Becomes Able to Imagine

This is where the developmental story becomes larger than play itself.

The child who once encountered the world primarily as something present can increasingly entertain what is absent.

What happened?

What might happen?

What if?

What else could this be?

What does the other person know?

What could we pretend?

The transition is from responding to reality toward representing reality and then manipulating the representation.

That does not mean that a three-year-old possesses an adult imagination.

It does not mean that fantasy is unrestricted.

It does not mean that imagination automatically produces creativity.

And it does not mean that pretend play is a magical training program for every desirable human capacity.

It means something subtler.

The mind is becoming capable of operating on possibilities.

Play provides one of the clearest environments in which that ability can be seen.

---

## Science, Interpretation, Art

**Science**

By age three, many children can use objects, actions, language, roles, and narratives to represent situations beyond the immediate present. Symbolic play becomes increasingly elaborate as representational, linguistic, social, motivational, and executive capacities develop.

The evidence supports play as an important developmental context. It does not establish play as a unitary cause of broad developmental outcomes.

**Interpretation**

The developmental importance of play may lie less in the possession of any particular skill than in the changing relationship between reality and possibility.

The child is becoming able to take something that exists and imagine it otherwise.

That transformation provides a common structure for exploration, representation, storytelling, rehearsal, negotiation, and eventually many forms of human thought.

**Art**

Play is one of the first places where a human being learns that reality can be inhabited in more than one way.

The block can remain a block and become a car.

The room can remain a room and become a forest.

The child can remain a child and become a doctor, a parent, a monster, a teacher, a king.

Nothing outside the imagination needs to change for the experience to become real in a different sense.

That is the quiet power of play.

It does not remove the child from reality.

It teaches the child that reality can be represented, rearranged, shared, and imagined otherwise.

And once a human being can imagine otherwise, a new kind of development becomes possible.

The child can begin not only to learn the world,

but to construct meanings within it.
